    "rendered_prompt": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nLet $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}.\\]\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nTo find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we start by applying the method of the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality). The AM-GM Inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{a_1 a_2 \\cdots a_n},\n\\]\n\nwith equality if and only if \\(a_1 = a_2 = \\cdots = a_n\\).\n\nWe apply the AM-GM Inequality to the terms \\(x^3\\), \\(2y^3\\), and \\(4z^3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8(xyz)^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot (xyz) = 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot (xyz).\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot (xyz).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we obtain:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 6\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot xyz.\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by \\(xyz\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} \\geq 6\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to see if there exist positive real numbers \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) such that the equality holds in the AM-GM Inequality. Equality holds in the AM-GM Inequality if and only if all the terms are equal. Therefore, we need:\n\n\\[\nx^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(x^3 = k\\). Then \\(2y^3 = k\\) and \\(4z^3 = k\\). Solving these equations, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^3 = \\frac{k}{2}, \\quad z^3 = \\frac{k}{4}.\n\\]\n\nTaking the cube roots, we find:\n\n\\[\ny = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}}, \\quad z = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k + 2 \\cdot \\frac{k}{2} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{k}{4}}{x \\cdot y \\cdot z} = \\frac{k + k + k}{x \\cdot y \\cdot z} = \\frac{3k}{x \\cdot y \\cdot z}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x^3 = k\\), we have \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{k}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\nx \\cdot y \\cdot z = \\sqrt[3]{k} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}} = \\sqrt[3]{k \\cdot \\frac{k}{2} \\cdot \\frac{k}{4}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k^3}{8}} = \\frac{k}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3k}{x \\cdot y \\cdot z} = \\frac{3k}{\\frac{k}{2}} = 6.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nReview the solution above. Identify and correct any errors; if it is correct, retain its answer. Give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n",
